To simulate vintage tape wow & flutter (or chorus/vibrato effects), modulate the delay line's delayTime AudioParam using one or more Low-Frequency Oscillators (LFOs).
Modulating the delay time alters the read-head playback speed relative to the write head, inducing a continuous Doppler pitch shift:

$$\Delta f = -f_0 \cdot \frac{d}{dt}\tau(t)$$
where $\tau(t) = \tau_0 + A \sin(2\pi f_{\text{LFO}} t)$.
Signal Routing & Parameter Differences
Wow (Slow Drift): Low frequency ($0.2\text{ Hz} - 1.0\text{ Hz}$), higher amplitude ($\approx 1 - 5\text{ ms}$). Models motor speed fluctuations or uneven tape reels.
Flutter (Fast Shimmer): Higher frequency ($4\text{ Hz} - 15\text{ Hz}$), very small amplitude ($\approx 0.1 - 0.8\text{ ms}$). Models capstan friction and mechanical vibration.



Plaintext
[ LFO 1: Wow (0.5 Hz) ] ───> [ Wow Gain (Depth: ±2ms) ] ────┐
                                                             ├──> [ DelayNode.delayTime ]
[ LFO 2: Flutter (6 Hz) ] ─> [ Flutter Gain (Depth: ±0.3ms) ]┘


In the Web Audio API, connect the LFO output gain nodes directly to delayNode.delayTime.
